digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "C_cu_mechanism" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given deduction-rule from S23 and deduction-case from C_cu_rev, therefore photo enables reversible PSMT with atomic-scale control."];
  "C_cu_result" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from S19 and induction-common from S24, therefore this work presents atomic-scale evidence and proposes an example of PSMT between two distinct materials."];
  "C_cu_rev" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from S21 and induction-common from S22, therefore light triggers dramatic face changing that is reversible by thermal segregation."];
  "C_evidence_gap" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given deduction-rule from S6 and deduction-case from S7, therefore there is a critical gap in atomic-scale validation of PSMTs."];
  "C_importance" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given deduction-rule from S2 and deduction-case from S1, therefore PSMT is crucial for both applied and fundamental science."];
  "C_limitation" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from S5 and induction-common from S8, therefore existing PSMT research is strictly limited to intra-material phase transitions."];
  "C_nature" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from S3 and induction-common from S4, therefore PSMTs are generally temporary unless structural conversion occurs."];
  "C_surf_affect" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given deduction-rule from S10 and deduction-case from S11, therefore surface reconstruction profoundly impacts material behavior."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Use STM/STS measurements together with DFT calculations to demonstrate that light irradiation induces a reversible surface reconstruction on Cu(111), in which a semiconducting Cu2Se monolayer undergoes interlayer Se-Cu exchange to form a metallic close-packed Cu monolayer, thereby providing atomic-scale evidence for a photoinduced semiconductor-to-metal transition between two different materials.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A direct atomic-scale study of a light-driven surface reconstruction can connect structural atom exchange with a semiconductor-to-metal electronic transition and thereby test whether a photoinduced transition can occur between distinct material layers rather than only between phases of one material."];
  "R11" [label="(8,12,0) Surface reconstruction substantially affects material behavior and is critical for fundamental and applied research."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there is: The photoinduced semiconductor-to-metal transition (PSMT) has demonstrated significant potential in various applications."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Deduction-rule: If surface reconstruction refers to structural changes in the surface lateral atomic arrangement (likely accompanied by varied vertical interlayer spacing and surface dipole), then it significantly affects material properties."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently there is: This transformation significantly affects the chemical and physical nature of materials, playing a vital role in catalysis, sensing, surface engineering, device design, and biomedical technologies."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently there is: Regulating and understanding surface reconstruction is thus critical for both fundamental and applied research."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently there is: Herein, we present atomic-scale evidence for a photoinduced transition from a Cu2Se layer on top of Cu(111) to the single-atomic Cu layer."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Deduction-rule: If the PSMT reflects photodynamic mechanisms, photoinduced excitations and transition processes, and the relationship between material electronic and atomic structures, then PSMT is crucial for both fundamental and applied science."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently there is: Combining scanning tunneling microscopy and spectroscopy (STM and STS) with density functional theory (DFT) calculations, light irradiation triggers a dramatic “face changing” by sinking Se atoms to the subsurface, creating a new Cu2Se sublayer, and lifting partial subsurface Cu atoms."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently there is: Upon thermal activation, the transition can be reversed to the original Cu2Se surface via segregation."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Deduction-rule: If the photoinduced transition of the surface into an electronically excited state is crucial because the calculated Cu2Se-to-Cu transition barrier is significantly lower in the excited state than in the ground state, then light can enable the PSMT."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) Currently there is: This work proposes an example of PSMT between two distinct materials."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Induction-case: Specific case: In most cases, the PSMTs are just temporary and disappear as the irradiation is switched off."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Induction-common: Common pattern from literature: In rare cases, the transition can be sustained, usually by a conversion between a material’s crystalline and amorphous forms."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Induction-case: Specific case: PSMTs can induce changes in atomic structures (e.g., Peierls distortion) or just alter the electronic structures."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Deduction-rule: If evidence for these phase transitions has been primarily based on conductivity variations, diffraction spectra, or theoretical simulations, then a direct real-space proof at the atomic scale is missing."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently there is: A direct real-space proof at the atomic or molecular scale remains absent."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Induction-common: Common pattern: All of the reported PSMTs occurred between two structural phases of the same material."];
